k or other ISRs if interrupts are enabled.\n     - Since there are no disable/enable interrupt calls in the code snippet provided, we assume that this ISR can execute at any time.\n     - Hence, this write operation can occur at any point after the ISR is invoked.\n\n3. **Line 22: `__VERIFIER_assert(g2 <= 1);`**\n   - **Control Condition**: The assertion itself is a control condition.\n   - **Variable Value Tracking**:\n     - Initially, `g2` is `0`.\n     - If `svp_simple_617_001_isr_1` executes before this line, it modifies `g2` by first incrementing and then decrementing it.\n     - After both operations (`g2 = g2 + 1` followed by `g2 = g2 - 1`), `g2` remains unchanged.\n   - **Execution Path Analysis**:\n     - If the ISR does not execute between lines 21 and 22, `g2` remains `0`, and the assertion holds.\n     - If the ISR executes between lines 21 and 22, `g2` is temporarily incremented to `1` and then decremented back to `0`. In either case, the assertion holds.\n     - Therefore, this line will always be executed with `g2` being `0`.\n\n4. **Line 14: `g2 = tmp4;` in `svp_simple_617_001_isr_1`**\n   - **Control Condition**: No explicit control conditions guard this write operation.\n   - **Variable Value Tracking**:\n     - `tmp4` is assigned `g2 - 1` at line 13.\n     - `g2` is read at line 13.\n   - **Execution Path Analysis**:\n     - Similar to line 10, this write operation can occur at any point after the ISR is invoked.\n\n5. **Line 24: `__VERIFIER_assert(g2 == 0);`**\n   - **Control Condition**: The assertion itself is a control condition.\n   - **Variable Value Tracking**:\n     - Initially, `g2` is `0`.\n     - If `svp_simple_617_001_isr_1` executes before this line, it modifies `g2` as explained earlier.\n   - **Execution Path Analysis**:\n     - Regardless of whether the ISR executes or not, `g2` ends up being `0` because the ISR's operations cancel each other out.\n     - Therefore, this line will always be executed with `g2` being `0`.\n\n#### Step 2: **Pay Attention to Control Condition Changes**\n\n- There are no explicit changes to control conditions via interrupt handling since there are no `disable_isr()` or `enable_isr()` functions present in the code.\n\n#### Step 3: **Check Interrupt Status**\n\n- The code does not include any interrupt enable/disable calls. Therefore, we must assume that the ISR can be invoked at any point unless otherwise specified.\n\n#### Step 4: Generate the Final Report\n\nAfter analyzing all the read/write operations:\n\n- Line 21: Always executed.\n- Line 10: Can be executed at any time when the ISR runs.\n- Line 22: Always executed.\n- Line 14: Can be executed at any time when the ISR runs.\n- Line 24: Always executed.\n\nGiven that the assertions hold true under all scenarios due to the nature of the ISR operations (increment followed by decrement), there are no atomicity violations.\n\n```plaintext\nNo defects.\n```\n\n[Expert R2] (Refine the report based on judge feedback, re-check for missed or false defects.)\n```plaintext\nNo defects.\n```\n\n"
